1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,185
Every response begins with information. A call. A location. A decision. This

2
00:00:06,185 --> 00:00:11,770
is Vector: the dispatch and mobile data terminal interface for your emergency-service

3
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:17,355
simulator. Bring calls, incident assessment, searches and resource control into one workspace.

4
00:00:17,355 --> 00:00:22,940
Follow a fictional incident from the first alert to mobilisation, then into

5
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:28,525
the crew terminal. This is the current demonstration build. Welcome to Vector.

6
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:34,602
Start on Dispatch. The map provides the central workspace, with calls, live

7
00:00:34,602 --> 00:00:39,478
incidents and resources available in surrounding panels. The main navigation takes you

8
00:00:39,478 --> 00:00:44,355
to the nine nine nine call page, the mobilisation screen and Ground.

9
00:00:44,355 --> 00:00:49,232
Before starting, identify the selected incident reference. That reference provides context for

10
00:00:49,232 --> 00:00:54,108
the information and resource controls you are about to use.

11
00:00:55,308 --> 00:00:59,018
Your workspace is not limited to one screen. Use the pop out

12
00:00:59,018 --> 00:01:02,728
control to open a floating panel or the map in its own

13
00:01:02,728 --> 00:01:06,438
window. Move that window to another display using its operating system title

14
00:01:06,438 --> 00:01:10,147
bar. Keep the call on your main screen and the map alongside.

15
00:01:10,147 --> 00:01:13,857
This staged two-display animation illustrates the tested pop out behaviour. Dock back

16
00:01:13,857 --> 00:01:17,567
restores the panel.

17
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:22,560
When a new call arrives, the pop up banner brings it to

18
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:26,353
your attention even when the call panel is closed. Read the service,

19
00:01:26,353 --> 00:01:30,146
reported nature, location and waiting information before answering. The persistent alert strip

20
00:01:30,146 --> 00:01:33,939
provides another route back to waiting calls. In this demonstration, Test call

21
00:01:33,939 --> 00:01:37,732
produces an example arrival. It is not a connection to a telephone

22
00:01:37,732 --> 00:01:41,525
network.

23
00:01:42,725 --> 00:01:47,243
Answering opens the call handling page and removes that call from the

24
00:01:47,243 --> 00:01:51,762
unanswered stack. Check the active call identity, the reported problem and any

25
00:01:51,762 --> 00:01:56,280
caller details supplied. Do not assume missing information has been verified elsewhere.

26
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:00,798
The example concerns smoke issuing from a dwelling. Keep the distinction between

27
00:02:00,798 --> 00:02:05,317
what the caller has reported and what has actually been confirmed.

28
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:11,393
Next, look at the location source. The interface can display handset location

29
00:02:11,393 --> 00:02:16,270
information and other location references. Read the source and any accuracy information

30
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:21,147
alongside the caller's description. A marker identifies a possible location; it does

31
00:02:21,147 --> 00:02:26,023
not prove which building or entrance is involved. If sources disagree, resolve

32
00:02:26,023 --> 00:02:30,900
that disagreement before treating the address as a confirmed incident location.

33
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:36,585
The compact address control is designed for a quick, deliberate choice. Select

34
00:02:36,585 --> 00:02:41,070
the matching address, check the building number, street and locality with the

35
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:45,555
caller, then use Confirm address. Add useful access details in the separate

36
00:02:45,555 --> 00:02:50,040
field. The confirmed state should reflect a completed check, rather than simply

37
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:54,525
accepting whichever search result happens to appear first in the list.

38
00:02:55,725 --> 00:02:59,747
If the suggested address is wrong or ambiguous, expand Search or change

39
00:02:59,747 --> 00:03:03,769
address. Compare the available records and their supporting details, then return to

40
00:03:03,769 --> 00:03:07,792
the compact confirmation step. Similar street names, nearby properties and a different

41
00:03:07,792 --> 00:03:11,814
access point can all matter. For an unclear location, preserve the uncertainty

42
00:03:11,814 --> 00:03:15,836
in the record instead of making the display appear more precise than

43
00:03:15,836 --> 00:03:19,858
the information allows.

44
00:03:21,058 --> 00:03:25,074
The triage area groups questions around the nature of the incident, risks,

45
00:03:25,074 --> 00:03:29,089
casualties, access and caller updates. In this prototype, pressing Ask inserts a

46
00:03:29,089 --> 00:03:33,104
scripted question and reply into the dialogue. In a connected system, the

47
00:03:33,104 --> 00:03:37,119
operator would record the caller's actual response. Work through the relevant questions

48
00:03:37,119 --> 00:03:41,135
and distinguish a direct observation from information the caller heard from somebody

49
00:03:41,135 --> 00:03:45,150
else.

50
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:50,902
The dialogue provides a chronological account of the call. Use it to

51
00:03:50,902 --> 00:03:55,453
follow what has been asked and what new information changes your understanding.

52
00:03:55,453 --> 00:04:00,005
In our example, reports about a person inside require careful attribution. Unknown

53
00:04:00,005 --> 00:04:04,557
is different from no. Keep checking for meaningful changes, while avoiding repeated

54
00:04:04,557 --> 00:04:09,108
questions that add no useful information to the incident record.

55
00:04:10,308 --> 00:04:14,868
The question counter is a progress guide, not a requirement to finish

56
00:04:14,868 --> 00:04:19,428
every question before mobilising. This interface allows creation when the essential call,

57
00:04:19,428 --> 00:04:23,988
location and incident type checks are satisfied, even with questions remaining. That

58
00:04:23,988 --> 00:04:28,548
does not mean skipping those essential checks. It means you can progress

59
00:04:28,548 --> 00:04:33,108
the response while continuing relevant information gathering as the call develops.

60
00:04:34,308 --> 00:04:37,990
Use Escalate call when the assessment needs a higher priority or a

61
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:41,672
review. Record the reason so the change is understandable to the next

62
00:04:41,672 --> 00:04:45,354
operator. If the call already has the highest available priority, the interface

63
00:04:45,354 --> 00:04:49,036
can represent a request for supervisor review. In this prototype that request

64
00:04:49,036 --> 00:04:52,718
is recorded locally; it does not notify a real supervisor or another

65
00:04:52,718 --> 00:04:56,400
control room.

66
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:02,202
The Systems menu brings enquiries into a separate movable window. Choose the

67
00:05:02,202 --> 00:05:06,803
system that answers your current question rather than opening every tool. Address

68
00:05:06,803 --> 00:05:11,405
Check helps inspect an address record. Premises Risk and Incident History provide

69
00:05:11,405 --> 00:05:16,007
additional context in this demonstration. Keep the active incident reference in view

70
00:05:16,007 --> 00:05:20,608
when copying information between the call screen and an enquiry window.

71
00:05:21,808 --> 00:05:26,743
Premises information can help identify recorded access issues or hazards, while incident

72
00:05:26,743 --> 00:05:31,678
history shows earlier events associated with a location. Those records add context;

73
00:05:31,678 --> 00:05:36,613
they do not establish what is happening now. Compare the address carefully

74
00:05:36,613 --> 00:05:41,548
and distinguish historical information from the current caller's report. The records shown

75
00:05:41,548 --> 00:05:46,483
here are demonstration content, with live data connections still to be added.

76
00:05:47,683 --> 00:05:51,963
For a relevant policing enquiry, select P N C and choose the

77
00:05:51,963 --> 00:05:56,242
appropriate record view. Copy controls are intended to reduce retyping of registrations,

78
00:05:56,242 --> 00:06:00,521
names, numbers and identifiers. Check the value and search context before using

79
00:06:00,521 --> 00:06:04,800
it. The P N C screen here contains clearly fictional sample records.

80
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:09,079
It demonstrates the layout and copying workflow, not access to the Police

81
00:06:09,079 --> 00:06:13,358
National Computer.

82
00:06:14,558 --> 00:06:18,324
The A N P R page presents a vehicle read history. Compare

83
00:06:18,324 --> 00:06:22,089
the registration, time, camera location and direction, then select a row for

84
00:06:22,089 --> 00:06:25,854
its details. A historic read is not proof of the vehicle's current

85
00:06:25,854 --> 00:06:29,619
position. The example uses fictional training reads and has no camera image.

86
00:06:29,619 --> 00:06:33,385
It shows how a result could be inspected without claiming a live

87
00:06:33,385 --> 00:06:37,150
tracking connection.

88
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:42,372
Some calls need more than one service. In our fire example, the

89
00:06:42,372 --> 00:06:46,394
reported risk to people may also require an ambulance response in the

90
00:06:46,394 --> 00:06:50,417
scenario. Select the incident type, then use Add beside Ambulance to include

91
00:06:50,417 --> 00:06:54,439
that service alongside Fire. Review the updated attendance preview. Add is different

92
00:06:54,439 --> 00:06:58,461
from Pass over: it keeps the current call while including another service

93
00:06:58,461 --> 00:07:02,483
in this simulated attendance.

94
00:07:03,683 --> 00:07:07,900
After checking the location, incident type and multi-service attendance, select Create incident

95
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:12,117
and mobilise. Vector creates one incident reference and opens the allocation screen.

96
00:07:12,117 --> 00:07:16,333
Returning to the same call shows Incident created, with a separate option

97
00:07:16,333 --> 00:07:20,550
to view mobilisation. Continue updating that existing incident. Adding a second service

98
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:24,767
is not a reason to create another copy of the same incident

99
00:07:24,767 --> 00:07:28,983
from this call.

100
00:07:30,183 --> 00:07:34,902
On Mobilising, review each required attendance slot. Allocate a suitable available resource,

101
00:07:34,902 --> 00:07:39,620
checking its type and existing commitments. In this example, a resource is

102
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:44,338
placed into a matching slot while other requirements remain unfilled. A selected

103
00:07:44,338 --> 00:07:49,057
callsign means it has been allocated. It has not necessarily received instructions,

104
00:07:49,057 --> 00:07:53,775
accepted the task or started moving towards the incident.

105
00:07:54,975 --> 00:08:00,252
Send the mobilisation message, then monitor the acknowledgement sequence. Sent, received, accepted,

106
00:08:00,252 --> 00:08:05,528
turning out and mobile are separate stages. This prototype provides controls to

107
00:08:05,528 --> 00:08:10,805
simulate those crew replies. A lack of acceptance is visibly flagged after

108
00:08:10,805 --> 00:08:16,082
the demonstration timeout. Do not interpret that example timeout as a universal

109
00:08:16,082 --> 00:08:21,358
service standard; the connected system should apply its own configured operational rules.

110
00:08:22,558 --> 00:08:26,664
An incomplete attendance remains visible after the allocated resources are sent. Continue

111
00:08:26,664 --> 00:08:30,769
to track unfilled requirements and review the effect of commitments on available

112
00:08:30,769 --> 00:08:34,875
cover. Available units, attendance and county cover panels support different parts of

113
00:08:34,875 --> 00:08:38,981
that picture. Sending some resources should not make the remaining requirements disappear.

114
00:08:38,981 --> 00:08:43,086
The incident needs to remain understandable to the next operator taking over

115
00:08:43,086 --> 00:08:47,192
the desk.

116
00:08:48,392 --> 00:08:52,418
Back on Dispatch, select an incident and then choose a resource from

117
00:08:52,418 --> 00:08:56,445
its map marker or Scene Units row. The rugged M D T

118
00:08:56,445 --> 00:09:00,472
window opens with the callsign and incident reference clearly displayed. Actions are

119
00:09:00,472 --> 00:09:04,498
tailored to the resource. The window can be moved, resized or minimised,

120
00:09:04,498 --> 00:09:08,525
keeping the map usable while you work with that unit's controls.

121
00:09:09,725 --> 00:09:13,490
Choose an action and select the crew required for it. The preview

122
00:09:13,490 --> 00:09:17,256
checks its local crew availability and shows active tasks with a way

123
00:09:17,256 --> 00:09:21,021
to release those crew. The main simulation contains richer capability and equipment

124
00:09:21,021 --> 00:09:24,786
rules which still need connecting to this interface. The Incident and Messages

125
00:09:24,786 --> 00:09:28,551
tabs keep context close by; message sending here creates a local demonstration

126
00:09:28,551 --> 00:09:32,317
record only.

127
00:09:33,517 --> 00:09:38,693
To finish, review the incident reference, confirmed location, recorded risks, resource status

128
00:09:38,693 --> 00:09:43,870
and anything still outstanding. End the call when appropriate in the workflow;

129
00:09:43,870 --> 00:09:49,047
ending a call does not mean the incident is resolved. The sequence

130
00:09:49,047 --> 00:09:54,223
is receive, confirm location, triage, make relevant enquiries, create once, allocate, send

131
00:09:54,223 --> 00:09:59,400
and monitor. That is the core Vector journey demonstrated in this introduction.
